    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form",
    "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$6{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+72x+70y=59?$",
    "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+72x+70y & =59 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+72x-5{y}^{2}+70y & =59 \\\\ 6[{x}^{2}+12x]-5[{y}^{2}-14y] & =59 \\\\ 6((x+6)^{2}-36)-5[(y-7)^{2}-49] & =59 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-216-5(y-7)^{2}+245 & =59 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-5(y-7)^{2} & =59+216-245 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-5(y-7)^{2} & =30 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-6,7).$",
